---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Who Does the Work"
  description="Agentic Developer Relations is shared work. Which responsibilities DevRel leads, which belong to docs, platform engineering, security and product, and how to staff it."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Roles and responsibilities</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">Who Does the Work</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      Agentic Developer Relations is shared work. What DevRel leads, what
      belongs to docs, engineering, security and product, and how to
      staff it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-12">
    <h2>The shift upstream</h2>
    <p class="mt-5">
      In the traditional DevRel model, value was created through
      production: writing tutorials, giving talks, building demos, running
      events. The skill was in execution.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      As AI tools speed up the production of content, the scarce thing
      becomes judgment: knowing what developers are trying to do, what a
      correct result looks like, and what to tell the product team. The
      responsibilities below sit at that end of the work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0">
        <h2>Responsibilities, and who leads</h2>
        <p class="mt-5">
          Companies are organized differently, so treat this as a starting
          allocation to adapt. Where an Agent Experience team exists in
          engineering, it will lead more of these.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
                <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Responsibility</th>
                <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Leads</th>
                <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Contributes</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">The task list: what developers ask agents to do with the platform</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Support, Product</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Evaluation suite and graders</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel, or an Agent Experience team where one exists</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Platform engineering</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Monitoring what agents choose, and the public record that may influence it</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Marketing</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Feedback into the roadmap</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel brings the evidence; Product decides</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Platform engineering</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Community and maintainer policy on agent contributions</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Open-source office, Legal</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Relationships with the companies that build agents and models</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Partnerships</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Documentation source of truth, and delivering it in a form agents can fetch</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Docs</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Skills, plugins, and instruction files</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Docs and DevRel jointly</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Platform engineering</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">API errors, sign-in, limits, sandbox, CLI, MCP server</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Platform engineering</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">DevRel, with evidence from evaluations</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Agent traffic analytics and attribution</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Growth or data</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Docs</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Security review and provenance of agent-facing content</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Security</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Everyone who publishes it</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>What DevRel brings</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Engineering owns much of what determines how agents fare. DevRel's
        contribution is distinct, and is the part nobody else is placed
        to make:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-5 space-y-2">
        <li>It knows what developers are trying to do, which is where the task list comes from.</li>
        <li>It can judge whether a result is good, not merely whether it runs.</li>
        <li>It already carries feedback from developers to the product team.</li>
        <li>It holds the relationships with the community, and now with the companies that build agents.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The DevRel lead for agent experience</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        One person in DevRel should own this. The title matters less than
        the ownership; "Developer Advocate, Agent Experience" fits most
        ladders. Their work has three parts.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <strong>Evaluation.</strong> They own the task list and the
        evaluation suite, run it on each release and each new model, and
        track the <a href="/measurement/">scorecard</a> by model and by
        task.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <strong>Maintenance.</strong> When an API version ships, they check
        every agent-facing artifact for breakage. When the suite shows a
        recurring failure, they find the cause and test the fix with and
        without.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <strong>Feedback.</strong> Recurring agent failures are symptoms.
        Behind each is a documentation gap, an awkward API surface, or a
        missing machine-readable description. They turn symptoms into
        specific requests for docs and platform engineering, each with the
        evidence attached.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10">Skills profile</h3>
      <p class="mt-4">
        Deep platform expertise (non-negotiable) + systematic engineering
        rigor + enough data analysis to read evaluation results honestly +
        enough understanding of agent behavior to tell a platform problem
        from a model problem. This combination is currently rare. It
        describes the Developer Advocates who already do this work
        informally and call it "AI tool testing."
      </p>

      <Callout type="amber" label="What changed on this page">
        An earlier version named three new DevRel roles: the Agent
        Champion, the Documentation Architect for Agent Consumption, and
        the API Experience Designer. The Agent Champion is the lead
        described above. The other two describe work that usually belongs
        to docs and to platform engineering, so they appear here as
        responsibilities, not as DevRel job titles.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>When agents operate the product</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Agents that act on a platform at runtime, for a user, raise the
        stakes and shift the balance toward engineering and security.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Who</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">What they do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Platform engineering</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Scoped, delegated credentials. Spending and rate limits. Revocation. An audit trail. Operations that are safe to repeat. Recovery from half-finished work.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Security</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Reviews what agents are permitted to do, and every change to the content and tools agents consume.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Product</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Decides which operations agents may perform at all, and what needs a person to approve.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">DevRel</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Finds out what users are delegating to agents, documents the limits in plain terms, and reports where agents fail or overreach.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-2">
        <h2>Staffing by organization size</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
                <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Organization</th>
                <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Minimum viable</th>
                <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Full program</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Small (1–5 DevRel)</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">One practitioner owns this one day per week. A hand-run suite of 10 tasks. Agent-facing guidance kept in the docs.</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Grow and automate the suite. Make the role full-time when your own data justifies it: the agent share of your docs traffic, and the gap your first runs reveal.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Mid-size (6–15)</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A full-time DevRel lead for agent experience. Automated suite across several models. Each agent-facing artifact tested.</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Selection monitoring. Suite runs on every release. A named counterpart in docs and in platform engineering.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Large (15+)</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A small DevRel group working with an Agent Experience or platform team. The scorecard reported alongside community measures.</td>
                <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A published evaluation suite. Security review of agent-facing content. Runtime controls for agents that operate the product.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mt-8">
        <p>
          Community work keeps its own budget floor at every size. The two
          kinds of work are done by people who sit close together, and
          often by the same people.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-6">
          <a href="/for-devrel-teams/" class="font-semibold">
            Continue to: For DevRel Teams →
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
